 2`.\n\nWait, let me verify this against GNU's actual behavior again. When I run `echo \"FOOBAR 01;34\" | gdircolors --sh -`, it outputs an empty `LS_COLORS` assignment with exit 0, which means unrecognized keywords with values are silently ignored. Our code does the same thing by falling through, so that's correct. The empty output makes sense because the input has no recognized keywords or TERM lines, so there are no entries to add to `LS_COLORS`. Our implementation matches this—when entries is empty, we return an empty string and output the LS_COLORS assignment with an empty value.\n\nBut I just realized the actual bug: unknown keywords *without* values should trigger a \"missing second token\" error, not be silently ignored. And there's another discrepancy—GNU exits with code 1 when it encounters an error, but our code exits 0. Let me test a few edge cases to confirm the exact behavior, like what happens with a bare extension like `.tar` that has no value assigned.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8948,"cache_read_input_tokens":30966,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8948},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f137de95-6108-4bc1-8d19-8cb1a4988912","uuid":"fcd45f93-7c8c-4c9f-bc6e-4fe7ca5e5803"}
